 fills with hot water. An opposite-type utensil falls into the accumulated water. What does the taught case say to do?","choices":["The utensil is automatically forbidden and must be kashered.","The whole sink becomes a כלי ראשון, so everything in it is forbidden.","Leave the utensil in place until the water cools.","Treat the accumulated sink water as כלי שני for this case; the utensil is permitted and should then be rinsed with cold water."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":28,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all that apply. Which are course leniency routes for hot faucet water traveling through pipes?","choices":["The water may be below יד סולדת בו.","Ohr L’Tzion treats travel through pipes as weakening to כלי שני.","Mixing hot and cold can further weaken the water.","Soap can be פוגם.","Travel through pipes makes the water stronger and therefore automatically gives it כלי ראשון status."],"answer":["A","B","C","D"]},{"id":29,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"truefalse","prompt":"True or False: If an opposite-type spoon is later found in the wrong drawer, the course rule permits it and uses a ספק-ספיקא rather than routine kashering.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":["A"]},{"id":30,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"single","prompt":"Under the course S”A rule about ash in hot wash water, when does the leniency apply?","choices":["Only if the ash is added after the dishes have absorbed the opposite taste.","Any time ash is added before the dishes are dried.","Only if the ash is added after the wash cycle but before rinsing.","Only when the ash was already present before the dishes or tastes entered the water."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":31,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all that apply. Which statements are part of the course dishwasher framework?","choices":["The spray can be treated as עירוי.","Rav Moshe permits sequential meat/dairy use with separate racks.","Soap can make residue פגום.","Opposite actual residue may not touch the current dishes.","Filters cannot retain actual food, so the filter never affects the analysis.","Filter/food residue and the timing of soap can change the outcome."],"answer":["A","B","C","D","F"]},{"id":32,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"single","prompt":"After a meat dishwasher cycle, you find an opposite-type metal fork inside. Which statement matches the course summary?","choices":["Rav Ovadya permits; Ashkenazic practice in the notes often treats kasherable metal after 24 hours as kasherable, while the other dishes are not automatically forbidden.","The opposite fork and all the other dishes are automatically forbidden and require corrective treatment.","Rav Ovadya forbids the fork, but Ashkenazic practice permits everything without any further step.","All dishes become forbidden unless the machine had separate racks."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":33,"category":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers","type":"truefalse","prompt":"True or False: As taught in the course, Dunkin was preferred over Starbucks because of the described washing systems and non-kosher cross-use, but that guidance was explicitly presented as time-specific rather than a timeless statement about current equipment.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":["A"]},{"id":34,"category":"Open Foods & Storage","type":"single","prompt":"Open milk was placed beside salt that is intended for meat. Later, the salt is not clumped and there is no evidence that milk entered it. What is the course rule?","choices":["The salt is automatically dairy and may not be used with meat.","It is fine to arrange the milk beside the salt this way לכתחילה.","Do not set it up that way; בדיעבד, under these stated facts, the salt may be used with meat.","The salt may be used only if it is transferred to a new container."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":35,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"Which status chart is correct?","choices":["יין נסך: rabbinic drinking prohibition only; סתם יינם: biblical drinking and benefit prohibition; מגע עכו״ם: always permitted for benefit.","יין נסך and סתם יינם are both biblical, while מגע עכו״ם is rabbinic.","יין נסך: biblical, drinking and benefit prohibited; סתם יינם: rabbinic, drinking and benefit prohibited under the original decree; מגע עכו״ם: rabbinic, with exact drinking/benefit status depending on who touched and how.","All three categories have exactly the same status in every case."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":36,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"Which attribution of the reason for prohibiting benefit from סתם יינם is correct?","choices":["Beit Yosef says the benefit ban was added only after people became lax; Ran says Chazal modeled סתם יינם exactly on יין נסך; Rashba says it was to prevent benefit from actual יין נסך.","Beit Yosef says Chazal modeled סתם יינם on יין נסך; Ran says the concern was benefiting from actual יין נסך; Rashba says the first decree targeted drinking and the benefit ban was added later when people became lax around libation wine.","Beit Yosef and Ran both say only intermarriage mattered, while Rashba says benefit was always biblical.","All three give the same reason."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":37,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"When ordinary non-Jews no longer libate wine, which comparison of course positions is correct?","choices":["Rashi/Geonim permit benefit from both a non-Jew’s own wine and Jewish wine touched by a non-Jew; Rosh still prohibits benefit from the non-Jew’s own wine but permits benefit from the touched Jewish wine; Rambam prohibits benefit in both cases.","Rashi/Geonim prohibit benefit in both cases; Rosh permits both; Rambam permits only touched Jewish wine.","Rosh and Rambam both permit benefit from a non-Jew’s own wine but not touched Jewish wine.","All three positions permit benefit from both categories."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":38,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"A Jew wants to make routine profit by selling, gifting, storing/aging, or renting space/services for prohibited wine. Which statement best matches the course S”A/Rama framework?","choices":["Under S”A these are benefit problems; the Rama provides a loss/בדיעבד route in appropriate cases, but the course framework does not turn that into a routine profit model.","S”A permits all of these activities לכתחילה, while the Rama forbids them.","Both S”A and Rama permit routine profit because drinking, not benefit, is the only remaining concern.","Selling may be a benefit problem, but storing/aging and renting space are permitted לכתחילה because no one is drinking the wine."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":39,"category":"סתם יינם — Foundations & Benefit","type":"single","prompt":"A Jewish-owned store sells both kosher and non-kosher wine. You buy a kosher bottle. Which payment method avoids the course concern about receiving proceeds fro